Inheritance
    Inherit the field and behaviour of the parent class to child class is called Inheritance.
    Analogy: A child inherits features from parents (surname, eye color).
In Java-
    A child class gets the parent’s fields and methods for free, so you don’t rewrite code.

    by using keyword "extends"
    Terms to know:
    Parent = superclass = base class
    Child = subclass = derived class
    extends = the keyword that creates the link

What happens in memory
    When you write new Dog(), one single object is created on the heap.
    It contains the parent’s fields (name) plus the child’s fields.
    There is no separate Animal object inside it.

    Heap:  [ Dog object: name (from Animal) + Dog's own fields ]
    Stack: d  ───────────►  (points to that Dog object)

Types of inheritance in Java

Type	                Example	                            Supported?
Single	                B extends A	                            ✅
Multilevel	            C extends B, B extends A	            ✅
Hierarchical	        B and C both extend A	                ✅
Multiple (classes)	    C extends A, B	                        ❌
Hybrid	                mix of the above                ✅ only via interfaces

Why no multiple inheritance? (Diamond problem)
    If class C extended both A and B, and both had a show() method,
    which one would C inherit? That ambiguity is the diamond problem.
    Java avoids it for classes. A class can implement multiple interfaces instead.
